N={
"altwlkany25_interspeech":("A speech system should not degrade one language or gender more than another when audio passes through a codec.","Transcoding changes the signal before recognition or sentiment analysis, and the codec may interact with language and vocal characteristics.","Treat a codec as a neutral pipe and measure only average speech quality.","Transcode millions of multilingual files through PSTN, VoIP, and neural codecs, then compare quality by language and gender.","The study measures speech quality after representative codec paths across more than two million multilingual files.","The target is a distribution of quality differences, not one overall mean; grouping by language and gender exposes unequal channel loss.","PSTN codecs show strong gender bias and neural codecs introduce language bias in the reported analysis.","Codec set, languages, gender labels, quality measure, and files define the boundary; causal mechanisms and mitigation in deployed networks remain open."),
"deng25_interspeech":("An ASR system should adapt online to an elderly speaker it has not seen, without stopping for a large offline retraining job.","Elderly speakers vary acoustically and linguistically, and one prompt that helps one speaker can hurt another.","Use one speaker-independent model or fine-tune a separate model per speaker.","Cluster speaker prompts into experts and let a router mix acoustic and language prompts for the new speaker at run time.","MOPSA uses K-means speaker prompt clusters and a router around Whisper, with separate acoustic and language-level prompts, tested on English and Cantonese elderly speech.","The router is a mixture-of-experts choice; WER/CER measure recognition and real-time factor measures adaptation cost.","The paper reports relative WER/CER reductions of 4.21% and 5.40% and up to 16.12x real-time speedup over offline adaptation.","Datasets, elderly populations, prompt clusters, and Whisper versions bound the claim; broader disorders, languages, and online failure recovery remain open."),
"cumani25b_interspeech":("A speaker verifier should combine several recognizers while producing scores that mean the same thing across operating thresholds.","Different recognizers make dependent errors and their scores have different scales, so adding scores can miscalibrate decisions.","Use a weighted linear score sum and tune it on one development set.","Model the joint score distribution with flexible marginals and a copula that captures dependency, then fuse and calibrate scores.","Variance-Gamma marginals describe each recognizer's score distribution and a Gaussian copula describes dependence for target and non-target trials.","The copula separates marginal shape from dependency; Cllr measures calibration and discrimination of verification scores.","On NIST SRE 2019 and SITW, the method reports up to 7% relative Cllr reduction versus discriminative linear fusion.","Datasets, recognizer diversity, score distributions, and calibration protocol bound the result; new speakers, channels, and attacks need separate evaluation."),
"bolanos25_interspeech":("A listener should be able to see which moments of an audio clip drove a classifier, especially when the model may be using a spurious sound.","There is usually no ground-truth explanation, so explanation methods can look plausible while pointing to the wrong time region.","Trust a saliency plot or compare explanations by visual appeal.","Create time annotations for target events as a proxy reference and benchmark model-agnostic post-hoc explanations against them.","The benchmark compares temporal explanation methods for audio classifiers and uses the annotations to expose spurious correlations.","Time-localized overlap turns an explanation into a measurable alignment problem; the proxy is useful but is not a causal proof of model reasoning.","The paper reports near-perfect explanations for some methods and shows their use in finding spurious correlations.","Event annotations, task type, explanation method, and proxy definition bound the claim; faithfulness under distribution shift remains open."),
"gallego25_interspeech":("A translation system should translate speech into text for languages with little or no labeled speech data.","Sound-to-meaning transfer crosses languages and modalities, while phonetic evidence is easier to share than complete translated speech examples.","Train a large speech-to-text translator only on languages with parallel speech and translation labels.","Insert phoneme recognition and phoneme-aware chain-of-thought steps, then gradually shift training from text support toward speech.","A multilingual LLM processes speech and phonemes under a curriculum; multilingual benchmarks compare low-, zero-, and high-resource settings.","Phonemes form an intermediate representation that can transfer sound structure across languages, while translation quality measures the final meaning transfer.","The paper reports improved low-resource translation and zero-resource operation, with a small high-resource tradeoff.","Languages, phoneme recognizer, curriculum, and benchmark define the boundary; zero-resource claims depend on what other language information is available."),
"geng25b_interspeech":("A person who cannot speak reliably may need a system that turns brain activity into a chosen voice without collecting data from the target speaker first.","EEG is noisy and indirect, while voice identity is highly speaker-specific; conversion must align these signals without target examples.","Train a voice converter only from speech or require target-speaker recordings before conversion.","Align EEG features with speaker voice features and use a speech-trained zero-shot voice-conversion model.","A three-stage training strategy maps EEG to speaker-specific features and conditions a pretrained speech-only converter; Dutch single-word production tests the system.","The alignment is a cross-modal mapping problem; zero-shot evaluation tests whether target identity can be supplied without target speech data.","The paper reports reliable target-voice conversion on the Single-Word-Production Dutch-iBIDS dataset.","Single words, EEG setup, target voices, and small dataset define the claim; intelligibility, privacy, consent, and real assistive communication remain open."),
"hou25b_interspeech":("A voice assistant should be judged not only by whether it understood a request but also by whether its spoken response sounds natural and conversational.","Semantic accuracy can hide stiff timing, poor prosody, or unpleasant acoustic quality, and existing tests emphasize understanding.","Score a voice assistant with text-task accuracy alone.","Build a benchmark that tests general knowledge, speech recognition/understanding, semantic generation, and acoustic generation together.","SOVA-Bench compares speech LLMs across comprehension and generated-speech dimensions, making acoustic quality an explicit evaluation target.","The benchmark separates what the assistant knows, what it understood, and how it sounded; no single score can substitute for those dimensions.","The paper presents a systematic evaluation framework intended to guide speech-LLM voice interaction.","Benchmark tasks, prompts, listeners, model versions, and acoustic measures define the comparison; long-term interaction quality and user adaptation remain open."),
"futami25_interspeech":("A speech-to-speech translator should inherit text-trained language knowledge without needing a huge paired speech-to-speech corpus.","Text-only LLMs know language patterns but not how discrete speech units carry them, and limited paired data makes direct adaptation unstable.","Fine-tune directly on speech units and expect the text model to discover the modality bridge.","Interleave aligned text and speech units during training, then gradually reduce the text proportion so the model moves toward speech output.","LLaMA3.2-1B is fine-tuned on CVSS with scheduled interleaving and evaluated across translation directions and resource levels.","The schedule is a curriculum over modalities: text provides a stable scaffold early, while speech units become responsible later; translation quality measures the endpoint.","The paper reports consistent translation improvements, especially in limited-data languages.","CVSS, unitizer, schedule, model size, and languages bound the result; naturalness, speaker identity, and unseen domains need separate tests."),
}
